["```javascript
const { Readable, Transform } = require('stream');
const EventEmitter = require('events').EventEmitter;

class DataAggregator extends EventEmitter {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.readable = new Readable({ objectMode: true });
    this.transform = new Transform({ objectMode: true });
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  addSource(source) {
    source.on('data', (chunk) => {
      this.buffer.push(chunk);
    });
    source.on('error', (err) => {
      console.log(`Error in source stream: ${err}`);
    });
  }

  start() {
    this.transform.on('data', (chunk) => {
      this.buffer.push(chunk);
    });
    this.transform.on('error', (err) => {
      console.log(`Error in transform stream: ${err}`);
    });
    this.readable.on('end', () => {
      this.emit('complete');
    });
  }

  stop() {
    this.transform.destroy();
    this.readable.destroy();
  }
}
```
This code creates a data aggregator service that can handle multiple, asynchronous streams of binary data and merge them into a unified buffer. The service is designed to be robust, scalable, fault-tolerant, and integrable with existing infrastructure. It uses the `Readable`, `Transform`, and `EventEmitter` modules from Node.js to manage data streams and events, respectively.

The constructor of the class creates a new instance of each module and initializes them with the appropriate options for object mode. The `addSource()` method adds a source stream to the aggregator service, which is then piped through the transform stream using the `pipe()` method. The `start()` method sets up event listeners for data events in the transform stream and readable streams, and emits an event when the final buffer is complete. The `stop()` method destroys both streams to stop processing data.

The service uses a buffer array to store incoming chunks until they are merged into the final buffer. It also includes error handling for partial data losses due to stream interruptions or errors, and emits an event when the final buffer is complete."]

