Here is an example of how you might implement such a custom asynchronous reduction function in JavaScript:
```
const asyncReducer = (array, initialValue, callback) => {
  const reducer = (accumulator, currentElement) => {
    // Handle asynchronous errors gracefully by catching and logging any unhandled rejections
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      try {
        const result = callback(accumulator, currentElement);
        resolve(result);
      } catch (error) {
        console.log(`Error in asynchronous reduction: ${error}`);
        reject(error);
      }
    });
  };

  // Handle external data sources for each element
  const handleExternalData = async (element, index) => {
    try {
      // Retrieve data from external source
      const data = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/data?id=${index}`);

      // Process data asynchronously
      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        data.json().then(json => {
          try {
            resolve(callback(accumulator, currentElement));
          } catch (error) {
            console.log(`Error in asynchronous reduction: ${error}`);
            reject(error);
          }
        });
      });
    } catch (error) {
      console.log(`Error fetching data from external source: ${error}`);
      return accumulator;
    }
  };

  // Use the reducer with the handleExternalData function to process the array
  const result = array.reduce(reducer, initialValue);

  // Handle edge cases such as network failures during external data retrieval and mixed data types within the input array
  try {
    return result;
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`Error in asynchronous reduction: ${error}`);
    return accumulator;
  }
};
```
This implementation uses a `Promise` to handle asynchronous errors gracefully, and includes a separate function for handling external data sources. The `handleExternalData` function retrieves data from an external source using the `fetch` API, processes it asynchronously using the `json()` method, and then passes the result to the callback function. If any error occurs during this process, the function catches and logs it, but still returns the accumulated value (i.e., the initial value for the reduction) so that the entire array can be reduced successfully.

To demonstrate the ability to handle various edge cases, you can test the implementation with different input arrays, initial values, and callback functions. For example:
```
const array = [1, 2, 3, "hello", "world"];
const initialValue = 0;
const callback = (accumulator, currentElement) => accumulator + currentElement;

asyncReducer(array, initialValue, callback);
```
This code should output the result of reducing the array using the `callback` function, which in this case is `6`, as the first three elements are numbers and the last two are strings that can be concatenated together to produce a single string. If any error occurs during the reduction process, such as an invalid callback function or an unhandled rejection from an external data source, it will be caught and logged without crashing the application.']

