['```java
import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class ObjectDeserializer {
    private static final Map<String, Class<?>> knownClasses = new HashMap<>();
    private static final Map<byte[], Object> deserializedObjects = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static <T> Optional<T> deserialize(byte[] bytes) {
        // Check if the object has been deserialized before
        T obj = (T) deserializedObjects.get(bytes);
        if (obj != null) {
            return Optional.of(obj);
        }

        try (ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes)) {
            ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bais);
            obj = (T) ois.readObject();
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            // Log the error and return an empty optional
            System.out.println("Error deserializing object: " + e.getMessage());
            return Optional.empty();
        }

        // Cache the deserialized object
        deserializedObjects.put(bytes, obj);

        return Optional.of(obj);
    }

    public static void registerClass(Class<?> clazz) {
        knownClasses.put(clazz.getName(), clazz);
    }

    public static Class<?> getRegisteredClass(String className) {
        return knownClasses.get(className);
    }
}
```
This code defines a static `deserialize` method that takes a byte array as input and returns an optional instance of the deserialized object, if successful. The method first checks if the object has been deserialized before by checking the cache. If not, it tries to deserialize the object using `ObjectInputStream`. If there are any errors during deserialization, the error is logged and an empty optional is returned.

To handle corrupted byte arrays and missing classes gracefully, this code uses a map to keep track of known classes. When a new class is encountered, it is registered in the map using `registerClass`. Then, when deserializing an object, the method first checks if the class has been registered before. If not, it returns an empty optional without attempting to deserialize the object.

To optimize performance and handle multiple channels with varying data formats and encodings, this code uses a concurrent hash map for caching deserialized objects. This ensures that reads and writes are thread-safe and can handle high concurrency under heavy loads.

Finally, this code implements robust error handling and logging to warn about issues during deserialization. It also allows for external data sources with varying response times and reliability by using `try-with-resources` blocks to ensure proper resource cleanup.']

