1. Memory Management

To optimize memory usage and minimize garbage collection overhead, we can implement a custom memory pool allocator using off-heap memory to bypass Java heap space limitations. The allocator will allocate memory in larger chunks and manage the allocation of smaller objects within those chunks. This will reduce fragmentation and improve cache hit rates.
```c
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

class CustomMemoryPool {
 public:
  CustomMemoryPool(size_t size) : size_(size) {}

  void* Allocate(size_t bytes) {
    if (bytes > size_) {
      throw std::bad_alloc();
    }

    auto& slot = slots_.emplace_back(new char[bytes]);
    return slot.get();
  }

 private:
  const size_t size_;
  std::vector<std::unique_ptr<char[]>> slots_;
};
```
2. Concurrency Control

To support concurrent access from multiple threads and processes without introducing race conditions or deadlocks, we can use fine-grained locks to protect critical sections of code. However, this can lead to contention and performance bottlenecks if the number of locks is too high. To mitigate this, we can use read-write locks to allow multiple readers but block writers until all readers have released their locks. We can also use lock-free data structures such as atomic variables and spinlocks to avoid contention.
```c
#include <mutex>
#include <shared_mutex>
#include <atomic>

class Cache {
 public:
  bool Insert(long key, int value) {
    std::unique_lock<std::shared_mutex> lock(mutex_);
    cache_.emplace(key, value);
    return true;
  }

  int Get(long key) {
    std::shared_lock<std::shared_mutex> lock(mutex_);
    auto it = cache_.find(key);
    if (it == cache_.end()) {
      return -1;
    }
    return it->second;
  }

 private:
  std::unordered_map<long, int> cache_;
  std::shared_mutex mutex_;
};
```
