The `parseConfig(config)` function parses the configuration file and returns a struct containing all relevant information. This function can be modified to handle different types of configuration files and different parsing mechanisms.

The `Connection` struct represents a single connection, which stores the status and error code of the connection as well as any additional metadata required for the specific use case. The `update(params)` method updates the connection with new parameters and handles any necessary reinitialization or resource management.

The `compileAndMonitor()` function starts a monitoring thread that continuously checks for updates to the configuration file and updates the necessary resources accordingly. If an update is detected, it will release the lock on the mutex before sleeping for a short period of time to avoid busy waiting. This ensures that any updates to the configuration file are processed efficiently while minimizing the impact on ongoing operations.

Once the monitoring thread has finished its initialization, the function starts the compilation process using the `Command` struct from the standard library. It uses the `--target` flag to specify the target architecture and the `output()` method to retrieve the status code, duration, and resident set size of the process.

Finally, the function handles any errors that may have occurred during the compilation process by logging them using the `logger()` function. If no errors were encountered, it will log a successful compilation message and return the exit code, duration, and resident set size of the process.']

