The function initializes several variables to manage memory allocation and data processing: `bufferSize` is the size of the internal buffer used to hold incoming data, `numRecordsProcessed` keeps track of the number of records processed so far, `file`, `netStreamFd`, and `dmqFd` are file descriptors for the local file, network stream, and distributed message queue, respectively.

The function then enters a loop that continues until all data has been processed and the buffer is empty. It checks if any data is available on the file, network stream, or distributed message queue using `dataAvailable` and breaks from the loop if no data is available. If data is available, it reads the data into the buffer using `fgetc`, `fscanf`, and `read`.

The function then processes and aggregates the data in the buffer by looping over each record and computing the average, maximum, and minimum metric values for that batch. It generates an aggregated report at the end containing the average, maximum, and minimum metric values per batch, along with the total number of records processed.

The code also includes several error handling mechanisms to gracefully handle memory allocation failures and failed connections to external data sources using `feof`, `malloc`, `free`, and `robust_error_handling` macros.']

