    for (int i = 0; i < maxSize; i++) {
        offsets[i] = rand() % rangeStart + rangeEnd;
        keys[i] = rand() % rangeStart + rangeEnd;
        records[i].key = keys[i];
        records[i].value = (double)rand() / RAND_MAX; // Random value between 0 and 1
        records[i].timestamp = time(NULL);
    }

    // Implement caching mechanism with a doubly linked list
    Cache *cache = malloc(sizeof(Cache));
    cache->size = maxSize;
    cache->count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < maxSize; i++) {
        cache->records[i] = records + i;
    }

    // Implement filtering mechanism to only consider records within the given interval
    time_t currentTime = time(NULL);
    int intervalStart = currentTime - 30 * 24 * 60 * 60; // 30 days ago
    int intervalEnd = currentTime + 30 * 24 * 60 * 60; // 30 days from now

    // Iterate through the records and filter out those that fall outside the given interval
    for (int i = 0; i < maxSize; i++) {
        Record *record = cache->records[i];
        if (record->timestamp < intervalStart || record->timestamp > intervalEnd) {
            continue; // Skip this record
        }
        if (!(record->key >= rangeStart && record->key <= rangeEnd)) {
            continue; // Skip this record
        }
        cache->count++;
    }

    // Implement caching eviction policy to prevent cache from exceeding its limit
    while (cache->count > maxSize) {
        Record *leastRecentlyUsed = cache->records[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < maxSize; i++) {
            if (cache->records[i]->timestamp < leastRecentlyUsed->timestamp) {
                leastRecentlyUsed = cache->records[i];
            }
        }
        int index = leastRecentlyUsed - records;
        free(leastRecentlyUsed); // Free the memory allocated for this record
        cache->count--;
        memmove(cache->records + index, cache->records + index + 1, sizeof(Record) * (maxSize - index)); // Shift the remaining records
    }

    // Clean up any allocated resources and return the cache
    free(offsets);
    free(keys);
    free(records);
    return cache;
}
```
The `processAndCacheRecords` function takes three arguments: `maxSize`, `rangeStart`, and `rangeEnd`. It allocates memory for three arrays: `offsets`, `keys`, and `records`. The `offsets` array is used to store the offsets of each record, while the `keys` array is used to store the keys of each record. The `records` array is used to store the actual records with their values and timestamps.

The function initializes the `offsets`, `keys`, and `records` arrays with random values within the specified range. It then creates a doubly linked list to manage the order of least recently used items in the cache. The list is implemented using a pointer to the first element in the list, which points to the second element, and so on.

The function then implements a caching mechanism that stores records in the cache based on their timestamps. It also includes a filtering mechanism that only considers records whose timestamps fall within a given interval from the current time, and ensures that the record's key falls within a specified range. The caching eviction policy prevents the cache from exceeding its limit by freeing the memory allocated for the least recently used record and shifting the remaining records in the list.

