The `MonitoringConfiguration` class is responsible for managing the monitoring configuration. It provides methods for updating configurations at runtime and applying changes immediately across all active monitoring agents. The `updateConfig` method acquires a lock on the mutex to ensure that only one thread can access the configuration at a time, and updates the configuration using the new_config parameter. The `applyChanges` method also acquires the lock on the mutex and iterates over all active monitoring agents, updating their configurations using the current monitoring configuration stored in the static member variable of the `MonitoringConfiguration` class.

The `MonitoringAgent` class is responsible for managing the monitoring agent's configuration. It provides methods for updating the configuration at runtime and applying changes immediately across all active monitoring agents. The `updateConfig` method acquires a lock on the mutex to ensure that only one thread can access the configuration at a time, updates its own configuration using the new_config parameter, and signals the condition variable to wake up any waiting threads. The `applyChanges` method also acquires the lock on the mutex and updates the monitoring agent's configuration using the current monitoring configuration stored in the static member variable of the `MonitoringConfiguration` class.

The system is designed to handle high volumes of concurrent configuration updates gracefully, minimizing contention and ensuring data consistency. To achieve this, a background worker thread processes and routes alerts asynchronously, allowing the main monitoring engine to remain responsive while avoiding performance impact.

